// CotSC Batch 18 - extracted original English dialogue for qchalin.d
// Keep existing @ numbers stable when translating.
@1 = ~Howdy, travelers! Haven't seen you in Beregost before. What brings you to this bustling town?~
@2 = ~We are on our way to Nashkel to solve the mines crisis... if we ever make it.~
@3 = ~We are simple travelers, weary after our travels.~
@4 = ~What do you care?~
@5 = ~Aaach, the mines. It's on the lips of every single adventurer nowadays. I have heard that the majority of people who make it inside are never seen or heard from again. 'Tis sad news indeed. You deserve a pint, for luck on your mission.~
@6 = ~In that case, have a seat and let's drink to luck and success on your endeavors.~
@7 = ~Whoa, don't shoot the messenger. I'm only trying to be friendly. Here, have a pint. Take a load off.~
@8 = ~My name is Halindar Droun — a local bard and the only source of entertainment in these parts, unless you count Silke Rosena of Berdusk, who arrived in town recently. Her voice is truly magnificent, although her personality upon meeting her tells you a lot about what it's like to interact with her. As a professional writer and singer, I believe in humility, and that's one thing she is lacking.~
@9 = ~I lived here my whole life. Seen things, heard things. You could almost say I am a chronicle of this town. I know every piece of history that applies to Beregost.~
@10 = ~If you need any information while you're here, you know where to find me.~
@11 = ~What would you like to know?~
@12 = ~Anything interesting happening in the area?~
@13 = ~As a bard who has lived here all your life, surely you have some interesting knowledge about Beregost.~
@14 = ~I think I'll be going now.~
@15 = ~As of late, the majority of news that reaches my ears concerns Nashkel or Ulcaster. I'm sure Ulcaster has reached your ears too. Would you like to hear about its demise?~
@16 = ~Yeah, with pleasure.~
@17 = ~I'd rather hear about something else.~
@18 = ~Sit back and listen up. Our story begins in the mid-eleventh century, with a conjurer named Ulcaster. Engaged in the art of magic, Ulcaster's school grew in popularity and attracted pupils from all over the Sword Coast and beyond. During the school's growth, Ulcaster was aided by Aumvor. Some call him Aumvor the Undying — a powerful lich of renowned powers.~
@19 = ~He was an ancient Netherese lich and archmage whose mastery of necromancy and dark magic became almost legendary. Before he became a lich, it is believed he existed for centuries, absorbing power from his servants, even members of his own family. He was ruthless, vicious, and power-hungry. Ulcaster did not realize that Aumvor was a lich using him as a pawn for his own agenda.~
@20 = ~Aumvor dreamed of restoring his fallen empire's grand traditions of high-level magic, arcane research, and unrestricted spellcasting. He deliberately left ancient Netherese grimoires, advanced magical research, and spell scrolls in places where he knew talented candidates would "accidentally" discover them. He hoped the academy would serve as a breeding ground to experiment with and awaken dormant Netherese bloodlines. Ulcaster had no clue.~
@21 = ~By planting this knowledge, Aumvor seeded ideas into Ulcaster's mind, steering him toward establishing an academy on the Sword Coast that would serve Aumvor's ultimate vision — until the unprecedented event occurred. The school's popularity caused certain parties to believe it had become too dangerous to leave alone. A group of Calishite mages intervened, and a magical battle exploded.~
@22 = ~Many school inhabitants lost their lives, including Ulcaster himself. The building was completely devastated, leaving naught but the ruins of a once-wonderful, ambitious academy for young mages and conjurers. Some say Ulcaster himself can still be seen or heard near the school's ruins, and that his spirit never passed to the afterlife due to the catastrophic destruction of his life's work, which left him with unresolved grief and pain. A talented spellcaster ended alone in the dust. A sad tale, indeed.~
@23 = ~Fascinating and sorrowful story. Couldn't have been easy on the town.~
@24 = ~Most boring tale I have heard all day.~
@25 = ~Actually, the town shook it off relatively quickly. Obviously, the incident reshaped Beregost in some ways. Some people grew afraid that phantoms from the ruins would come to torment us in their eternal afterlife, and the administration ended up in a massive vacuum. To fill this void, the grand Song of the Morning temple, dedicated to Lathander, was built. Armed acolytes of the Morninglord took over the town's day-to-day administration and now maintain the peace in the absence of an official government. It is what it is, as they say.~
@26 = ~No story will ever satisfy every audience.~
@27 = ~Amongst plenty of such tales, I could tell you of the most troubling one that plagues my mind once in a while, due to the severity of the situation it produced. Once, before 1370 DR, Beregost had another settlement situated less than three hours away from here. Not just any settlement, but an Illithid settlement.~
@28 = ~You might recognize the name Illithids. Commoners refer to them as mind flayers. An ancient, intellectually advanced race from the far future who escaped the heat death of their original universe by traveling backward through time and space. One particular group of them, named the Ariser Creed, founded the city of Tellectus in the Underdark. The majority of Beregost's citizens didn't even realize of their existence, and those who knew kept things quiet to avoid spreading moral panic.~
@29 = ~At some point, a group of courageous adventurers raided Tellectus, slaughtered many mind flayers, and discovered certain tablets which allowed safe passage to Oryndoll — City of Lore-Takers, one of the earliest and grandest mind flayer bases, filled with exclusive knowledge that rivaled even Candlekeep's library. Moreover, the theory was that they attempted to document Candlekeep's defenses and obliterate the citadel, then obtain the knowledge within. Due to the raid, these plans were abandoned by the Arisers, but were later continued from Oryndoll, though nothing came of it.~
@30 = ~Once the threat was eliminated, the news reached Beregost and made people more afraid than relieved. Those who hadn't known about the mind flayers found out and perceived them as a potential future threat that might arise again. With time, rumors of their return circulated again, but gradually died off. Now, most people don't think about it anymore.~
@31 = ~Sounds ominous. Great tale, indeed.~
@32 = ~I expected better.~
@33 = ~And my favorite, despite its chilling reputation.~
